From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Date: Thu, 23 Oct 2026 22:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: nf_flow_table: keep TTL on flows bridged within one
 bridge

A flow that enters and leaves through two ports of the same bridge never
leaves L2, so a bridge must not consume a hop's worth of TTL for it.  The
hardware side already knows this (the airoha PPE driver clears BIND_TTL for
such flows), but nf_flow_table_ip.c decremented unconditionally:

	ip_decrease_ttl(iph);      (IPv4 forward)
	ip6h->hop_limit--;         (IPv6 forward)

On this target a flow only becomes a hardware entry after about 31 packets
(REG_PPE_BIND_RATE is 0x1e), so every bridged flow lost a hop on its first 31
packets before the hardware took over: with the sender at TTL 5, 29 of the
first 31 packets were captured arriving with TTL 4.

Both endpoints of an offloaded tuple are bridge ports, because the flowtable
hooks ingress on the ports rather than on the bridge master.  Compare the two
masters and skip the decrement when they are the same.  Packets with TTL 1
never reach this code: they are rejected during tuple building.

This patch lives under airoha/patches-7.3 rather than generic/pending-7.3
because 915-01 quotes the same two lines as context.  Generic patches are
applied first, so the decrement still has to be there when this one runs.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_ip.c
@@ -497,6 +497,36 @@
 				       other->encap[0].id);
 }
 
+/* A flow that enters and leaves through two ports of the same bridge never
+ * leaves L2, so it must be forwarded without touching the IP TTL.  This
+ * mirrors what the airoha PPE driver does when it programs the hardware
+ * entry; without it the packets that still traverse the software fast path
+ * before the hardware entry is installed lose a hop.
+ *
+ * Both endpoints of the tuple are bridge ports here: the flowtable hooks
+ * ingress on the ports themselves, not on the bridge master.
+ */
+static bool nf_flow_bridged_within_one_bridge(struct nf_flowtable_ctx *ctx,
+					      struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash *tuplehash)
+{
+	const struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple = &tuplehash->tuple;
+	struct net_device *outdev, *indev;
+	bool bridged = false;
+
+	if (!netif_is_bridge_port(ctx->in))
+		return false;
+
+	rcu_read_lock();
+	outdev = dev_get_by_index_rcu(ctx->net, tuple->out.ifidx);
+	indev = dev_get_by_index_rcu(ctx->net, tuple->iifidx);
+	bridged = outdev && indev && netif_is_bridge_port(outdev) &&
+		  netdev_master_upper_dev_get_rcu(outdev) ==
+		  netdev_master_upper_dev_get_rcu(indev);
+	rcu_read_unlock();
+
+	return bridged;
+}
+
 static int nf_flow_offload_forward(struct nf_flowtable_ctx *ctx,
 				   struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
 				   struct flow_offload_tuple_rhash *tuplehash,
@@ -546,7 +576,8 @@
 				    FIELD_PREP(INET_DSCP_MASK,
 					       tuplehash->tuple.dscp));
 
-	ip_decrease_ttl(iph);
+	if (!nf_flow_bridged_within_one_bridge(ctx, tuplehash))
+		ip_decrease_ttl(iph);
 	skb_clear_tstamp(skb);
 
 	if (flow_table->flags & NF_FLOWTABLE_COUNTER)
@@ -1150,7 +1181,8 @@
 				    FIELD_PREP(INET_DSCP_MASK,
 					       tuplehash->tuple.dscp));
 
-	ip6h->hop_limit--;
+	if (!nf_flow_bridged_within_one_bridge(ctx, tuplehash))
+		ip6h->hop_limit--;
 	skb_clear_tstamp(skb);
 
 	if (flow_table->flags & NF_FLOWTABLE_COUNTER)
